## INTRODUCTION
The coexistence of two or more ferroic orders in the same material, such as electric and magnetic ordering, i.e., multiferroicity, has attracted a lot of interest in the past decade.1 Most of the known multiferroics are inorganic compounds.2 However, in recent years, the search for new ferroelectrics and multiferroics has been extended to include purely organic compounds.3,4 A combination of these two classes of materials has opened a new route to achieve multiferroicity, e.g., by considering hybrid organic−inorganic compounds such as metal−organic frameworks (MOFs).5−15
MOFs are porous crystalline materials made up of inorganic and organic building blocks, whose cavities can be filled by organic cations.16 They have been intensively studied for a number of applications such as gas storage, catalysis, molecular recognition, drug delivery, and other medical applications.17−20 This wide range of applications is a result of their dual nature, namely benefiting from the characteristics of both the inorganic and organic building blocks. For magnetic MOFs, it is possible to control the nature of magnetic coupling due to the variety of possible metal ions, short-ligands, coligands, templates, or even radical ligands carrying a spin degree of freedom.21 MOFs with perovskite $\mathrm{AB}\mathrm{X}_{3}$ architecture are particularly interesting because some of them show a hydrogen-bonded-related multiferroic behavior.6,7 The perovskite topology also represents a similarity to the inorganic materials, to which most known multiferroics belong.
A new series of $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ MOFs such as $\left[\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{3}\right]\left[\mathrm{X}-\$ $\left(\mathrm{HCOO}\right)_{3}]$ , where $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Fe, Co, $\mathrm{Ni,}$ and $\mathrm{Cu,}$ have been successfully synthesized.16 The Cu-based MOF shows a multiferroic and magnetoelectric behavior with an estimated saturated polarization of $0.37~\mu\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ .6 Recently, a novel Crbased multiferroic and magnetoelectric MOF has also been designed from first-principles. For the first time in the MOF family, a hybrid improper nature of ferroelectricity has been shown, because the coupling of two nonpolar modes induces a polar behavior.22−28 In the $\mathrm{Cr}$ -MOF, at variance with the inorganic case,22 the coupling of nonpolar distortions is realized by Jahn−Teller pseudorotations29 and tilting of the molecular A group. The “glue” which mediates this coupling is the hydrogen bondin g.7,30 Interestingly, it has been predicted that a large weak-ferromagnetic moment (up to ${\sim}1\ \mu_{\mathrm{B}}/\mathrm{Cr}$ atom) is coupled to the ferroelectric polarization. The latter, however, is still small $(\sim0.22~\mu\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ because it arises from an induced dipole moment on the nonpolar A-group organic cation. However, in MOF compounds, it is possible to exploit the large variety of A or $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ organic groups to tune the multiferroic properties. Here we show how to tune the polarization of $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ type MOFs by carefully choosing the organic A cation.
We performed density functional simulations on a new MOF, $\mathrm{\bar{[{CH_{3}C H_{2}N H_{3}}]}{M n}}[\mathrm{({HCOO})_{3}}]$ .31 Here A is ethylammonium $\mathrm{[CH_{3}C H_{2}N H_{3}]^{+}}$ , $\mathbf{B}$ is the divalent $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ ion, and X is the carboxylate $[\mathrm{HCOO}]^{-}$ . Hereafter, we refer to it as the Mn-MOF. We started our calculations from experimental crystallographic data,32 optimizing the structure until Hellmann−Feynman forces were smaller than $0.02\ \mathrm{eV}/\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . Kohn− Sham equations were solved using the projector augmentedwave (PAW) method using the Heyd−Scuseria−Ernzerhof hybrid functional33 as implemented in VASP.34,35 The energy cutoff for the plane wave expansion was set to $400~\mathrm{eV},$ ; a $3\times4$ $\times\ 2$ Monkhorst−Pack grid of $k$ -points was used. In the Supporting Information (SI), we present systematic convergence tests with respect to energy cutoff and $k$ -point sampling. Furthermore, calculations based on different van der Waals functionals are reported to study the effects of weak interactions on the current system. However, no significant differences were found, demonstrating that the main results presented in this study are robust with respect to different approximations used.
## STRUCTURAL AND MAGNETIC PROPERTIES
The crystal structure of Mn-MOF is composed of an anionic $\mathrm{\DeltaNaCl}$ -framework of $[\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3}^{-}]$ where the nearly cubic cavities are occupied and charge-counter-balanced by the ethylammonium $\mathrm{\hat{[{CH_{3}C H_{2}N H_{3}}]^{+}}}$ cations. The compound belongs to the polar space group $P n a2_{1},$ and therefore a spontaneous polarization is allowed by symmetry. Each manganese ion is connected to its six nearest neighbors by bridging ligands. In the lattice, the $_\mathrm{Mn-HCOO-Mn}$ linkages are along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis and along the two diagonal directions of the ab plane. The $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ groups of the ethylammonium cation form $\mathrm{N-H\cdotsO}$ and $\mathrm{C-H\cdotsO}$ hydrogen bonds with the oxygen atoms of the framework. The octahedrally coordinated $\mathrm{Mn}^{\dag+}$ imoantse $\mathrm{\Phi^{\circ}d^{s}}$ twhiet $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathrm{g}}^{3}\mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{g}}^{2}$ ef scltirgohtnliy cisotnofirtgeud toicotnah) lriea with equatorial $\mathrm{Mn{-}O_{\mathrm{eq}}}$ bond lengths ranging from 2.17 to $2.23\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and axial $\mathbf{Mn}{-}O_{\mathrm{ax}}$ bond-lengths of 2.17 and $2.22\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ The octahedra are tilted about the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis by ${\sim}30^{\circ}$ . The Mn-MOF can then be viewed as composed of chains running along the caxis: within each chain, the Mn units are connected by axial $\mathrm{HCO}_{\mathrm{ax}}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ax}}$ groups, whereas parallel chains are linked by equatorial $\mathrm{HCO_{\mathrm{eq}}O_{\mathrm{eq}}}$ groups. The Mn sites are coupled antiferromagnetically (AFM) both in the $a b$ plane and along the $c$ -axis, so that the ground state displays a G-type AFM spin configuration. The magnetic moment associated with each Mn ion is about $4.5~\ \mu_{\mathrm{B}},$ consistent with the $\mathsf{d}^{\mathsf{S}}$ electronic configuration. Mapping the system onto a Heisenberg model $