## FERROELECTRIC PROPERTIES
It is important to introduce a reference centric structure, in terms of which the low symmetry structure can be described by small amplitude symmetry breaking distortions.36 In this way, it is possible to analyze atomic displacements responsible of the polarization. In Figure 1a we compare the paraelectric to the ferroelectric phase. As expected, the main atomic displacements involve the organic A groups as shown by oval lines, while very small distortions affect the octahedra (see curved arrow). Note that the A-group cation is polar due to the presence of polar bonds. In fact, the $\mathrm{C-N}$ bond is polar because the electronegativity of C and N are 2.6 and 3.0, respectively.37 Also the $\mathrm{{N-H}}$ bonds are polar (H has 2.2 as electronegativity).37 Simple geometrical consideration shows that the moments do not compensate, leaving a dipole moment mainly along the $_{\mathrm{C-N}}$ bonds. Explicit calculation for the isolated cation gives a dipole moment of 0.78 eÅ. (We consider the isolated A cation in a box of $25\times25\times25\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ . Convergence with respect to box’s dimensions has been carefully checked.) In the centric structure $\left(\lambda=0\right)$ , the A group is constrained to have the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-C-N}$ bonds in the $a b$ plane. In particular, the Agroup dipole moments belong to planes parallel to the $a b$ plane as shown in Figure 1b: within each plane, there is weak ferroelectric ordering, which is coupled antiferroelectrically with the nearest planes. The resulting polarization is therefore zero (see Figure 1b). In the polar phase $\left(\lambda\ =\ 1\right)$ , the ethylammonium molecules is bent out of the $a b$ planes, i.e., the $C-$ $_{\mathrm{C-N}}$ bond tilts (see Figure 1c). The resulting weakferroelectricity is not compensated anymore along the $\mathbf{\Psi}_{c}$ axis and a net dipole moment arises (see Figure 1c). The polar phase is found more stable than the centric one by about 0.38 eV/(f.u.) formula unit.
